"A computer cannot guess what you mean.": "7b5c32ed9ac2",
"In Lesson 6 you watched a processor fetch an instruction, carry it out, and fetch the next one. But where do the instructions come from? People write them. A list of instructions written for a computer is called a program. An encyclopedia describes a program as an unambiguous, ordered sequence of instructions. Two words there matter most: ordered, and unambiguous.": "13aaea2d6788",
"In Lesson 6 you watched a processor fetch an instruction, carry it out, and fetch the next one. But where do the instructions come from? People write them. A list of instructions written for a computer is called a program. An encyclopedia describes a program as an unambiguous, ordered sequence of instructions. Two words there matter most: ordered, and unambiguous. Think of a small robot on a floor of squares. It understands only three instructions: forward, turn left, and turn right. To send it to a flag, you write the instructions one under another, and the robot carries them out from top to bottom. The order matters. “Forward, then turn left” and “turn left, then forward” end on different squares. The robot does exactly what is written. If you leave out one “turn right,” it does not notice. It carries on and finishes somewhere else. If you tell it to go forward when a wall is in the way, it walks into the wall. A mistake in a program is called a bug, and finding and fixing it is called debugging. When a program goes wrong, it is usually because the computer did just what it was told. Often a program does the same thing again and again. To climb four stairs, the robot does “forward, turn left, forward, turn right” over and over. Written out one by one, that is a long list. It is better to write those four once and say “repeat 4 times.” This is called a loop. The program becomes shorter, and it is easier to read and to change. Sometimes the right move depends on what the robot finds. So a program can ask a question: “If there is a wall ahead, turn left.” The robot checks, and it does the inside part only when the answer is yes. A program with a question in it can work in rooms its writer has never seen. In the same way, a real program can wait to see which key you press. These are the three building blocks: doing things in order, repeating, and choosing. Real programs are written in programming languages such as Python, and then they are translated, in several stages, into the machine language of Lesson 6. A game or a web browser may have millions of lines. But line by line, it is still someone’s instructions, carried out exactly.": "7cb49ce8d551",
"Often a program does the same thing again and again. To climb four stairs, the robot does “forward, turn left, forward, turn right” over and over. Written out one by one, that is a long list. It is better to write those four once and say “repeat 4 times.” This is called a loop. The program becomes shorter, and it is easier to read and to change.": "0cecd6c198dc",
